# ...but the built WASM package IS committed, deliberately.
#
# The backend loads `pkg/infrazero_simulation_engine.js` at runtime, so a clone
# without it cannot run the simulation at all -- and rebuilding needs the Rust
# toolchain plus wasm-pack, which a reviewer cloning the repo will not have.
# The package is ~350KB.
#
# It cannot be re-included with a negation here: wasm-pack regenerates
# `pkg/.gitignore` containing `*` on every build, and a nested .gitignore wins.
# The five files were therefore added once with `git add -f`, and because
# .gitignore does not apply to already-tracked files, later rebuilds show up as
# ordinary modifications with no further flags.
#
# To re-add after a change of crate name or a fresh clone:
#   git add -f pkg/infrazero_simulation_engine.js #              pkg/infrazero_simulation_engine.d.ts #              pkg/infrazero_simulation_engine_bg.wasm #              pkg/infrazero_simulation_engine_bg.wasm.d.ts #              pkg/package.json
#
# This exception exists because the rules above had already caused the bug it
# now prevents: the only pkg files ever committed were an OLD build under the
# previous crate name, so a fresh clone silently ran a stale engine with none
# of the queueing behaviour.
